From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:30:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wbemdisp: Implement SWbemObjectPath, named values and
 privileges as Windows has them.

Measured on Windows 11 build 29671 with tools/winmgmtsprobe
(objectpath.vbs, pathobject.vbs, namedvalues.vbs, privileges.vbs,
dispids.c, classinfo.c):

- SWbemObject is an ISWbemObjectEx; SystemProperties_ lists __PATH
  first and __GENUS last.  Refresh_, GetText_ and SetFromText_ are
  stubs.
- WbemScripting.SWbemObjectPath can be created.  Its properties come
  from wmiutils' path, its setters change it, text that is no path
  leaves it as it was; Keys is a set of the keys as they are, which
  changes go to the path (a value put on a key changes it and is then a
  failure, as on Windows).  An object's own path is a new one each time
  and read-only.
- DisplayName writes WINMGMTS:, then {authenticationLevel, ...,
  impersonationLevel, authority, (privileges)} and [locale=...] when
  set, then ! and the path; setting it reads them back.  A path of its
  own has no levels until they are given, and reading one fails.
- The locator, a connection and an object keep their settings; a
  connection starts with the locator's, an object and its path with the
  connection's.  The winmgmts: moniker takes the settings, the locale
  and the authority.
- SWbemPrivilegeSet keeps privileges by identifier, from
  wbemPrivilegeCreateToken to wbemPrivilegeManageVolume, adds them by
  identifier or by their Se...Privilege name; SWbemPrivilege has its
  name and the system's display name for it.
- SWbemNamedValueSet counts, enumerates in order, clones, removes (a
  name that is not there is no failure, unless it is a path's key) and
  deletes; a named value reads the set's value as it is now.
- ExecQuery's results have their keys and paths, whatever the query
  selected; a method set counts its methods.

The tests check object paths, keys, display names and privileges; on
Windows they have no failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

